// LeetCode 312 — four arms against each other and against seven properties
// that follow from the STATEMENT rather than from any implementation.
//
//   A  bottom-up interval DP, nested table   — shortest interval first
//   B  the same recurrence, flat table       — same order, strided layout
//   C  the same recurrence, top-down memo    — no order chosen at all
//   D  exhaustive search over burst orders   — the statement, nothing added
//   E  the same recurrence, generic table    — a monomorphisation surface
//
// WHAT THE ARMS CANNOT SHARE. A, B and C are three schedulings of ONE
// recurrence, and they share its whole premise: that fixing the balloon burst
// LAST in an interval splits it into two independent halves. If that premise
// were wrong, all three would be wrong together and agree perfectly. Their
// individual bugs are scheduling bugs — A can iterate intervals in an order
// that reads unwritten cells, B can do the same through arithmetic that hides
// which row it is touching, C cannot get the order wrong at all but can
// confuse "computed 0" with "not computed" — and none of those is the premise.
//
// D IS THE ARM THAT ASSUMES NOTHING, and that is why it earns its O(n!). It
// maintains the row of surviving balloons and pays each burst against whatever
// is adjacent at the moment of bursting, so the coupling that makes the
// FORWARD framing fail — removing a balloon makes its neighbours adjacent —
// is present in it by construction rather than reasoned around. Every
// agreement between D and the others on n <= 8 is a check of the reframing
// itself, not of its implementation.
//
// P2 AND P6 ARE THE ONES NO ARM COMPUTES. Each relates SEPARATE invocations
// to one another, so each catches a fault that is symmetric across every arm —
// the failure mode all-arms agreement is blind to.
//
//   P2, reversal: bursting is symmetric under reversing the row, because a
//   balloon's pay depends on its two neighbours and not on which side they are
//   on. Every arm walks the row in one fixed direction and none of them knows
//   this. Measured: it fires on every one of the five falsifying mutations
//   below, at ~1800-2100 of 3024 cases each.
//
//   P6, the feasible lower bound: the answer is a MAXIMUM over orders, so it
//   is at least the total paid by any particular order. The harness bursts
//   left-to-right, simulating the statement directly, and asserts the arms are
//   never below it. This is the only property that specifically catches
//   UNDER-maximisation — a truncated candidate loop, or a recurrence whose
//   terms have gone negative — which is what a too-early table read produces.
//
// P5 IS NOT ONE OF THEM, AND IT LOOKS LIKE IT SHOULD BE. Monotonicity — with
// non-negative values, raising one balloon's number cannot lower the answer —
// reads like the strongest property here: it constrains how the answer must
// MOVE under a perturbation, which no arm computes even implicitly. It is also
// very nearly a TAUTOLOGY over the mutations that can actually be made.
//
// The reason is structural. The computed answer is a maximum over sums of
// products of non-negative values, and ANY expression of that shape is
// monotone in each of its inputs — the maximum of monotone functions is
// monotone. So monotonicity follows from the SHAPE of the computation and not
// from its correctness, and every single-line mutation preserves the shape:
// swapping indices, truncating the candidate loop, reading an unwritten cell,
// duplicating a boundary factor. All of them stay monotone while being wrong.
//
// Measured: P5 fired on NONE of the six single-line mutations, including one
// written specifically to be antitone (subtracting the boundary product). That
// one fails to break it because `best` starts at 0 and only ever moves up, so
// negative terms are clamped away and the answer collapses to 0 — which is
// monotone. Breaking P5 took TWO simultaneous mutations, subtracting the
// boundary product AND lowering the initial `best` past 0 so negatives
// propagate; then it fires on 1751 of 3024 cases. So P5 is falsifiable in
// principle and unfalsifiable by any plausible single slip.
//
// It is kept because it costs one call and it does pin the non-negativity
// clamp, which is a real invariant. It is NOT counted as an independent check,
// and a kata that presented it as one would be overstating its battery.

// ------------------------------------------------------------------- arm A

fn coins_a(nums: ref Vec[i64]) -> i64 {
    let n = nums.len();
    if n == 0 { return 0; }
    let mut a: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
    a.push(1);
    for i in 0..n { a.push(nums[i]); }
    a.push(1);

    let w = n + 2;
    let mut dp: Vec[Vec[i64]] = Vec.new();
    for i in 0..w {
        let mut row: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
        for j in 0..w { row.push(0); }
        dp.push(row);
    }
    for len in 2..w {
        for i in 0..(w - len) {
            let j = i + len;
            let mut best = 0;
            for k in (i + 1)..j {
                let coins = dp[i][k] + dp[k][j] + a[i] * a[k] * a[j];
                if coins > best { best = coins; }
            }
            dp[i][j] = best;
        }
    }
    return dp[0][n + 1];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------- arm B

fn coins_b(nums: ref Vec[i64]) -> i64 {
    let n = nums.len();
    if n == 0 { return 0; }
    let mut a: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
    a.push(1);
    for i in 0..n { a.push(nums[i]); }
    a.push(1);

    let w = n + 2;
    let mut dp: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
    for i in 0..(w * w) { dp.push(0); }

    for len in 2..w {
        for i in 0..(w - len) {
            let j = i + len;
            let ai = a[i];
            let aj = a[j];
            let base = i * w;
            let mut best = 0;
            for k in (i + 1)..j {
                let coins = dp[base + k] + dp[k * w + j] + ai * a[k] * aj;
                if coins > best { best = coins; }
            }
            dp[base + j] = best;
        }
    }
    return dp[n + 1];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------- arm C

struct Memo {
    a: Vec[i64],
    w: i64,
    seen: Vec[Option[i64]],
}

impl Memo {
    fn solve(mut ref self, i: i64, j: i64) -> i64 {
        if j - i < 2 { return 0; }
        let slot = i * self.w + j;
        match self.seen[slot] {
            Some(v) => { return v; }
            None => {}
        }
        let mut best = 0;
        let mut k = i + 1;
        while k < j {
            let coins = self.solve(i, k) + self.solve(k, j)
                      + self.a[i] * self.a[k] * self.a[j];
            if coins > best { best = coins; }
            k = k + 1;
        }
        self.seen[slot] = Some(best);
        return best;
    }
}

fn coins_c(nums: ref Vec[i64]) -> i64 {
    let n = nums.len();
    if n == 0 { return 0; }
    let mut a: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
    a.push(1);
    for i in 0..n { a.push(nums[i]); }
    a.push(1);
    let w = n + 2;
    let mut seen: Vec[Option[i64]] = Vec.new();
    for i in 0..(w * w) { seen.push(None); }
    let mut m = Memo { a: a, w: w, seen: seen };
    return m.solve(0, n + 1);
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------- arm D

fn brute(alive: Vec[i64]) -> i64 {
    let n = alive.len();
    if n == 0 { return 0; }
    let mut best = 0;
    for i in 0..n {
        let mut left = 1;
        if i > 0 { left = alive[i - 1]; }
        let mut right = 1;
        if i + 1 < n { right = alive[i + 1]; }
        let paid = left * alive[i] * right;
        let mut rest: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
        for j in 0..n {
            if j != i { rest.push(alive[j]); }
        }
        let total = paid + brute(rest);
        if total > best { best = total; }
    }
    return best;
}

fn coins_d(nums: ref Vec[i64]) -> i64 {
    let mut alive: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
    for i in 0..nums.len() { alive.push(nums[i]); }
    return brute(alive);
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------- arm E

// Arm B's recurrence and schedule behind a generic bounded table. Adds a
// MONOMORPHISATION surface, not an algorithmic one -- it cannot disagree with
// B for a reason about burst balloons, only for a reason about generics.

trait Zero {
    fn zero() -> Self;
}

impl Zero for i64 {
    fn zero() -> i64 { return 0; }
}

struct Table[T] {
    cells: Vec[T],
    w: i64,
}

impl[T: Zero + Copy] Table[T] {
    fn build(w: i64) -> Table[T] {
        let mut cells: Vec[T] = Vec.new();
        for i in 0..(w * w) { cells.push(T.zero()); }
        return Table { cells: cells, w: w };
    }
    fn get(ref self, i: i64, j: i64) -> T {
        return self.cells[i * self.w + j];
    }
    fn set(mut ref self, i: i64, j: i64, v: T) {
        self.cells[i * self.w + j] = v;
    }
}

fn coins_e(nums: ref Vec[i64]) -> i64 {
    let n = nums.len();
    if n == 0 { return 0; }
    let mut a: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
    a.push(1);
    for i in 0..n { a.push(nums[i]); }
    a.push(1);
    let w = n + 2;
    let mut dp: Table[i64] = Table.build(w);
    for len in 2..w {
        for i in 0..(w - len) {
            let j = i + len;
            let mut best = 0;
            for k in (i + 1)..j {
                let coins = dp.get(i, k) + dp.get(k, j) + a[i] * a[k] * a[j];
                if coins > best { best = coins; }
            }
            dp.set(i, j, best);
        }
    }
    return dp.get(0, n + 1);
}

// --------------------------------------------------------------- properties

// Burst strictly left to right, simulating the statement. One feasible order,
// so its total is a valid LOWER bound on the maximum.
fn left_to_right(nums: ref Vec[i64]) -> i64 {
    let n = nums.len();
    let mut alive: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
    for i in 0..n { alive.push(nums[i]); }
    let mut total = 0;
    while alive.len() > 0 {
        let m = alive.len();
        let mut right = 1;
        if m > 1 { right = alive[1]; }
        // The leftmost balloon always has the sentinel on its left.
        total += 1 * alive[0] * right;
        let mut rest: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
        for j in 1..m { rest.push(alive[j]); }
        alive = rest;
    }
    return total;
}

fn reversed(nums: ref Vec[i64]) -> Vec[i64] {
    let n = nums.len();
    let mut r: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
    let mut i = n;
    while i > 0 {
        r.push(nums[i - 1]);
        i = i - 1;
    }
    return r;
}

fn sum_of(nums: ref Vec[i64]) -> i64 {
    let mut s = 0;
    for i in 0..nums.len() { s += nums[i]; }
    return s;
}

fn max_of(nums: ref Vec[i64]) -> i64 {
    // At least 1, because the sentinels are 1 and bound the boundary terms.
    let mut m = 1;
    for i in 0..nums.len() {
        if nums[i] > m { m = nums[i]; }
    }
    return m;
}

fn make_row(n: i64, seed: i64, cap: i64) -> Vec[i64] {
    let mut state = seed;
    let mut v: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
    for i in 0..n {
        state = (state * 1103515245 + 12345) % 2147483648;
        if state < 0 { state = -state; }
        v.push(state % cap);
    }
    return v;
}

fn main() {
    let mut cases = 0;
    let mut p1 = 0;
    let mut p2 = 0;
    let mut p3 = 0;
    let mut p4 = 0;
    let mut p5 = 0;
    let mut p6 = 0;
    let mut p7 = 0;

    let mut n = 0;
    while n <= 8 {
        let mut cap = 2;
        while cap <= 20 {
            let mut round = 0;
            while round < 48 {
                let seed = 7919 * (n * 37 + cap) + round * 7717 + 1;
                let nums = make_row(n, seed, cap);

                let ra = coins_a(nums);
                let rb = coins_b(nums);
                let rc = coins_c(nums);

                // P1 — the four arms agree. D only for n small enough that
                // n! is affordable; A/B/C on every case.
                let re = coins_e(nums);
                if ra != rb or ra != rc or ra != re { p1 += 1; }
                if n <= 7 {
                    if coins_d(nums) != ra { p1 += 1; }
                }

                // P2 — reversal invariance. No arm computes this.
                let rev = reversed(nums);
                if coins_a(rev) != ra { p2 += 1; }

                // P3 — a lone balloon is paid 1 * x * 1.
                if n == 1 {
                    if ra != nums[0] { p3 += 1; }
                }

                // P4 — two balloons: burst one, the other is then flanked by
                // both sentinels. a*b + max(a, b), from the statement.
                if n == 2 {
                    let mut bigger = nums[0];
                    if nums[1] > bigger { bigger = nums[1]; }
                    if ra != nums[0] * nums[1] + bigger { p4 += 1; }
                }

                // P5 — monotonicity under raising one entry. No arm computes
                // this; it constrains how the answer MOVES.
                if n > 0 {
                    let idx = round % n;
                    let mut bumped: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
                    for i in 0..n { bumped.push(nums[i]); }
                    bumped[idx] = bumped[idx] + 1 + (round % 5);
                    if coins_a(bumped) < ra { p5 += 1; }
                }

                // P6 — the maximum is at least any feasible order's total.
                if ra < left_to_right(nums) { p6 += 1; }

                // P7 — every burst pays at most M * nums[i] * M, so the total
                // is at most M^2 * sum. A loose bound, but it fails loudly for
                // an arm that double-counts a balloon.
                let m = max_of(nums);
                if ra > m * m * sum_of(nums) { p7 += 1; }

                cases += 1;
                round = round + 1;
            }
            cap = cap + 3;
        }
        n = n + 1;
    }

    println(f"cases {cases}");
    println(f"P1 arm disagreements {p1}");
    println(f"P2 reversal failures {p2}");
    println(f"P3 single-balloon failures {p3}");
    println(f"P4 two-balloon closed-form failures {p4}");
    println(f"P5 monotonicity failures {p5}");
    println(f"P6 feasible-lower-bound failures {p6}");
    println(f"P7 upper-bound failures {p7}");

    if p1 == 0 and p2 == 0 and p3 == 0 and p4 == 0 and p5 == 0 and p6 == 0 and p7 == 0 {
        println("DIFFERENTIAL OK");
    } else {
        println("DIFFERENTIAL FAILED");
    }
}
